  "rationale": "The example is only partly covered by its one `{test}` reference, so I judge it not met.\n\n**Test reference.** The only test referencing `requirements.references.selector_same_line` is `tests/commands.rs::add_input_modes_capture_raw_sources_and_refresh_idempotently` (line 779).\n\n**What the test covers.**\n- It writes `fn main() { fn nested() {} }` on line 4 of `src/main.rs`, with doc and attribute lines above it.\n- It runs `add-input --for-requirement rules.ready --rust-item src/main.rs:4`.\n- It asserts that stdout contains both `fn[main]` and `fn[main]/fn[nested]`. That shows two separate selectors from one line, a parent and its nested child.\n- It asserts that the evidence file holds 3 inputs: the file-range input plus the two rust-item inputs.\n- It asserts that `inputs[1]` (the `fn[main]` input) has the complete raw text. That text includes the preceding doc, attribute and comment lines, plus the full line `fn main() { fn nested() {} }`.\n- A repeat run reports `unchanged` and leaves the file byte-identical.\n\n**Gaps.**\n1. **Missing assertion on the nested item.** The example says \"Both contain the complete raw line.\" The test never inspects `inputs[2]`, the nested `fn[main]/fn[nested]` input. It only checks its selector name in stdout. Nothing verifies that the nested item's stored text or line range holds the complete raw line.\n2. **Different scenario.** The example uses `impl Thing { fn run() {} }` and expects `impl[Thing]` and `impl[Thing]/fn[run]`. The test uses nested `fn`s instead. `src/rust_items.rs` has a unit test with `impl Thing { fn run() {} }` on one line, but it checks selectors and is not marked as a test of this example. It also does not exercise the `add-input` command or the stored raw lines.\n\nAdding an assertion on `inputs[2]`'s text, or marking a test that covers the `impl`/`fn` case end to end, would close the gap. I found no FIXME or other acknowledgement of this gap in the sources.",
